---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import map from "../data/map.json";

/*
 * The credibility page. It is what a careful reader and an answer engine both
 * check, so the figures in it are read from the same file the map is built
 * from rather than typed. It described the transaction-cost calculator until
 * September 2026, which the site no longer contains.
 */
const cs = map.communities as any[];
const sum = (map as any).summary;
const fmt = new Intl.NumberFormat("en-AE");
const asOf = new Date((map as any).latest).toLocaleDateString("en-GB", {
  day: "numeric", month: "long", year: "numeric",
});
const PERIODS = [
  { id: "12m", label: "twelve months" },
  { id: "3y", label: "three years" },
  { id: "5y", label: "five years" },
  { id: "10y", label: "ten years" },
];
---

<Base
  title="Method and sources"
  description="How Property Desk turns the Dubai Land Department transaction register into a community price map: the join, the thresholds, and what is excluded."
  path="/method"
>
  <div class="col" style="padding-block: var(--space-l) var(--space-xl);">
    <h1>Method and sources</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Everything on this site is computed from one file: the Dubai Land
      Department transaction register, published through Dubai Pulse open data.
      Nothing is an asking price, an estimate or a projection.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top: var(--space-l);">What is counted</h2>
    <p style="margin-top: var(--space-s);">
      {fmt.format((map as any).totalSales)} residential sales, registered up to
      {asOf}. A sale counts as residential when the register describes it as a
      studio, a numbered bedroom count or a penthouse. Shops, offices and land
      plots are excluded, because a price per square foot that mixes a shop with
      an apartment describes neither.
    </p>
    <p>
      Mortgage registrations are counted separately from sales. They are not a
      subset of them, which is why the financed share is expressed as
      registrations per hundred sales rather than as a percentage of buyers.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top: var(--space-l);">How a sale is matched to a community</h2>
    <p style="margin-top: var(--space-s);">
      This is the step that most often goes wrong. The transaction file carries
      an <code>area_id</code>, and the community boundary file carries a
      <code>COMM_NUM</code>. They look like the same number and they are not.
      Joining them directly produces roughly ninety coincidental matches whose
      names disagree with each other, which is how a map ends up confidently
      showing the wrong prices in the wrong places.
    </p>
    <p>
      The register's own area lookup is the bridge: <code>area_id</code> to the
      lookup's municipality number, and only then to the boundary file. Where a
      community is better known by another name, the familiar name is shown
      first and the official one beneath it, because nobody searches for Al
      Thanyah Fifth.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top: var(--space-l);">Medians, not averages</h2>
    <p style="margin-top: var(--space-s);">
      Every headline figure is a median. A handful of very large sales pulls an
      average upward, and in a market with as much variation as Dubai's the
      average of a community is frequently a number that no property there
      actually sold for.
    </p>
    <p>
      A community is only reported once it records at least 20 priced sales
      within the period being shown. Below that a single tower or a single land
      plot decides the answer. That threshold is the reason parts of the map are
      grey, and the reason longer periods fill it in:
    </p>
    <ul style="margin-top: var(--space-s);">
      {PERIODS.map((p) => (
        <li>
          Over {p.label}, {sum[p.id].reportable} of {cs.length} communities
          are reportable, from {fmt.format(sum[p.id].sales)} sales.
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top: var(--space-l);">How growth is measured</h2>
    <p style="margin-top: var(--space-s);">
      Each period is compared against the equally long period immediately before
      it. The twelve month figure is measured against the twelve months before
      that, and the ten year figure against the decade before that. A short
      window set against a fixed distant baseline would flatter every area the
      further back the baseline reached, and would hide a market that has turned
      recently.
    </p>
    <p>
      Large swings in recently launched communities usually reflect a change in
      what is being sold there rather than a repricing of the same stock. An
      area whose first towers complete will shift from land and off-plan studios
      to finished apartments, and the median moves with the mix.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top: var(--space-l);">What the numbers are not</h2>
    <p style="margin-top: var(--space-s);">
      A community median is not a valuation. It covers every building, age,
      floor and finish in that community at once, and the spread inside a single
      community is often wider than the gap between two communities. Nothing
      here tells you what a particular property is worth.
    </p>
    <p>
      The register records what was registered. It does not capture what was
      paid outside the recorded price, what incentives a developer attached, or
      what a payment plan was worth in present value. Off-plan sales in
      particular are registered at the contract price, which is not the same as
      a cash price today.
    </p>
    <p>
      Boundaries are simplified for drawing. They are accurate enough to
      identify a community and are not a cadastral record.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top: var(--space-l);">Corrections</h2>
    <p style="margin-top: var(--space-s);">
      Every figure carries the date of the most recent sale behind it, currently
      {asOf}. A market figure with no date is a market figure that is quietly
      wrong, and the date above is how you check whether this has been rebuilt
      since you last looked.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
